\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
At every fixed positive computable viscosity, can a finite machine and input effectively determine smooth mean-zero forcing on $T^3$, independent of time after $t=1$ and with every derivative globally bounded, whose zero-data Navier–Stokes solution is globally smooth, classically unique and has uniformly bounded energy, while one fixed particle enters one fixed open strip exactly if the encoded machine halts?

\subsection{Short English statement}
At every fixed positive computable viscosity, can a finite machine and input effectively determine smooth mean-zero forcing on $T^3$, independent of time after $t=1$ and with every derivative globally bounded, whose zero-data Navier–Stokes solution is globally smooth, classically unique and has uniformly bounded energy, while one fixed particle enters one fixed open strip exactly if the encoded machine halts?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/build/main.tex}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Universal-Computation-with-Eventually-Stationary-Navier-Stokes-Forcing-September-27-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

